Couple bones piles gave some decent loot, including a magic lamp which
I used for light.  Amulet of reflection and BoH too, so I didn't
bother opening the Sokoban zoo.

Coaligned altar in Minetown, so I sac'd like a mofo.  Got +1 Keolewa
and a +5 chain mail.

Around level 12, wraith-danced to XL 2 so I could buy protection.
Almost died due to not watching my HP.  Could have drained to 1, but I
didn't want to risk getting hit again accidentally.  Got to -2 natural
AC.  I had been searching for Ludios but it didn't seem to exist,
unfortunately.  Probably wouldn't have bothered level-draining if I
had found it.

An ogre dropped a +1 battle-axe, best weapon I could hope for thanks
to double Str bonus on 2-handers.

Got two wishes from a throne around dungeon level 18 or so. Orange DSM
and Gauntlets of Power.  Thus equipped, I went after Chromie, who went
down without a fuss.

Castle wand was 0:2, but I didn't use it right away.  Got a throne
wish at the castle, wished for a pet Archon. (named Ted, until I found
out it was a girl; renamed Sue.)  Speed boots in castle armory.

In Gehennom I made sure to keep my battle-axe blessed, in case of a
curse attack.

Demon lords didn't give me a problem, thanks to Mr. Tinfoil Hat (which
was a regular helmet this game, so I had loads of them) protecting me
against their uber Psi Bolt.  Sue didn't help due to their high level,
but the battle-axe felled them easily enough.  In Spork, I usually try
to bribe the lawful princes, but I was feeling lucky.

Somewhere along the way I wished for a wand of polymorph, to use on
the +5 chain mail I had received from Anu. Changed it into leather
gloves and saved them for later. The idea here, since I can't enchant/
fireproof/repair my armor, is to wear mediocre armor through Gehennom,
so the good stuff doesn't get trashed by fire traps and spellcasters.
Then I can put on the good armor on the way up.  I decided to put on
some jumping boots and save the speed boots for later too.

Sue did a great job of crowd control, and she took care of Vlad
easily.  Vlad's throne gave me a geno, which I used on arch-liches.
Hate those teleporty bastards.

Rodney put up a decent fight and damaged a lot of my armor. He is HARD
to hit when you don't have an enchanted weapon.

Sue tore through the Sanctum like nobody's business. Left me four
wraith corpses, too. Yummy.  She wouldn't touch the high priestess so
I took her on myself, using up several full healing potions in the
process.

An umber hulk confused Sue on the way up, and she started zapping me
with Touch of Death. :( I avoided her until she came to her senses.
(This is where I discovered her gender. "Ted casts a spell!  Oh no,
she's using the touch of death!")

Rodney came up fairly often, and in one encounter he double-troubled
four times. Stole my Sceptre, and since I didn't have another source
of MR I hurried up the stairs and wished up some gray DSM. Wished for
silver afterward, so I could doff the amulet of reflection in favor of
life saving.

Dispatched Rodney on Earth without too much difficulty. He came up
again on Air, stole the Amulet, and summoned (among other critters) a
flayer that sucked me down to 6 Int.  He also destroyed my +4 elven
cloak, so I rubbed my magic lamp and got +2 displacement.  Sue
disappeared through the portal in the middle of the fight, so it was
pretty rough getting the Amulet back.  Many full healing potions were
quaffed.

I had brought a crystal ball from the Castle up to find portals on the
Planes. Either it only had two charges, or after the mind-flaying I
was too stupid to use it; so for Fire, I used a wand of secret door
detection and found the portal in two zaps. Didn't have as much luck
on Water; had to wander around, during which Rodney popped in again
and caused more grief.  Eventually found the portal when another
creature vanished through it.

Astral was relatively easy thanks to Sue and the guardian Angel.  (I
decided not to use conflict around them.)  Took down Death with some
flint-tipped arrows. Famine gave me some trouble with his stun attack
while I was surrounded, but I eventually got in a few whacks with the
battle-axe. Sue took care of Pestilence for me on the way to the third
altar.

After playing illiterate so much, I have a new way of looking at a lot
of the game.  You can ID a lot of the important stuff without identify
scrolls -- engrave with wands, dip-test potions, drop rings in sinks
-- even amulets are fairly easy to sort out if you know they're not
cursed.  There's also the chance of getting an ID from a throne (I
pulled out all my unknown stuff whenever I sat on one).  The only
scrolls I really missed are enchant weapon/armor and genocide.  And
magic mapping for the maze levels, I suppose.

And boy, do illiterate cavemen get a bunch of HP.  Some of those 326
HP were from potions, but still.

Goodbye Dentarthurdent the Demigod...
